  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nRainy Friday\n\nWIth a ridge firmly in place over the southern Plains, disturbance \ncurrently over the Sierra Nevada looks to advance northeastward \nover the northern Plains and then southeast over our CWA on Friday. \nThis wave should amplify as it does so given upper support from a \nzonal upper jet over MB/ON and a weak upper jet advancing from OR to \nover NE. As this occurs, surface high developing in the OH Valley in \nthe wake of a slowly departing upper low currently over the \nNortheast Corridor will set up southerly winds across the \nMississippi Valley, leading to an increase in moisture. By 12z \nFriday, PWATs may (20-50%) exceed 2\" along and south/west of \nInterstate 94, close to the climatological maximum in the DVN RAOB \nrecord. This will lead to a swath of moderate rain from MN/IA into \nWI/IL. Within this swath, rain amounts will very likely (75-85% per \n29.06z NBM) exceed 1\" with a low risk (20%) to exceed 3\" and a tail \nrisk (1-5%) for a stray 5\" total. Main disagreement across guidance \nis on the exact north/south location of the upper wave and, \nconsequently, the location of higher rainfall amounts. GFS/GEFS \ncontinues to remain farther south - putting heaviest rain south of \nour CWA - compared to the EC and first available runs of the NAM are \nroughly in between these two camps. Given this and the richly moist \nair mass, not enough agreement across guidance on the position of \nthe upper low to discount a risk for moderate to heavy rain in our \nCWA, with the risk for this relatively higher across northeast Iowa \nand diminishing greatly along and north/east of Interstate 94. High \nresolution guidance should cover the entire rain period over the \nnext 1-2 update cycles so hopefully confidence in placement will \nincrease soon.\n\nFriday could also bring a risk for a stray tornado as southerly \nwinds at the surface underlay westerly winds at 850mb and above, \nreflected in quickly turning hodographs in the low levels with \nsufficient sfc-500m SRH for a tornado. However, multiple things will \nhave to come together to realize this - the main wave needs to \narrive in the afternoon during peak heating and precipitation in the \nsoutherly warm advection needs to have breaks in it. Otherwise, \ninstability will be minimal, an outcome borne out by virtually all \nmodel soundings which, unsurprisingly, stick with a close to moist \nadiabatic profile given the high end moisture setup. Will have to \nkeep this in mind, but the risk appears very low (<2%).",
